-aggregate-demand-and-aggregate-supply-analysis","2-3-4-possible-conflicts-between-macroeconomic-policy-objectives"],"board":"aqa","groups":["macroeconomics"],"keywords":["supply-side policies","long-run aggregate supply","price stability"],"qpPage":7,"msPage":3,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null,"type":"mcq","options":["A reduction in interest rates and increased government borrowing.","A reduction in the budget deficit and the rate of growth of the money supply.","Increased government expenditure on apprenticeships and transport infrastructure funded through borrowing.","Increased government expenditure on welfare funded through higher indirect taxes."],"usesFigure":true},{"id":"aqa-p2-2018-jun-q7","p":55,"section":"A","questionNumber":"7","parentQuestion":null,"choiceGroup":null,"marks":9,"questionText":"Extract E (lines 16–17) states ‘However, some argue that trying to maintain stable prices and low unemployment could create trade-offs.’ With the help of a diagram, explain why a trade-off between price stability and low unemployment might occur.","topics":["2-3-4-possible-conflicts-between-macroeconomic-policy-objectives"],"board":"aqa","groups":["macroeconomics"],"keywords":["policy conflicts","inflation","unemployment","trade-offs"],"qpPage":5,"msPage":11,"ctxPage":4,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"aqa-p3-2018-jun-q9","p":64,"section":"A","questionNumber":"9","parentQuestion":null,"choiceGroup":null,"marks":1,"questionText":"The short-run Phillips curve below illustrates the relationship between unemployment and inflation in an economy. The Phillips curve shows that in this economy","topics":["2-3-4-possible-conflicts-between-macroeconomic-policy-objectives"],"board":"aqa","groups":["macroeconomics"],"keywords":["Phillips curve","inflation and unemployment trade-off"],"qpPage":10,"msPage":3,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null,"type":"mcq","options":["high inflation leads to high unemployment.","there is an inversely proportional relationship between unemployment and inflation.","the trade-off for reducing inflation from 7% to 3% is a 4% increase in unemployment.","the trade-off for reducing unemployment from 7% to 4% is 3% inflation."],"usesFigure":true},{"id":"aqa-p2-2017-jun-q10","p":54,"section":"B","questionNumber":"10","parentQuestion":null,"choiceGroup":null,"marks":25,"questionText":"Discuss the view that falling unemployment will inevitably lead to trade-offs with other macroeconomic policy objectives.","topics":["2-3-4-possible-conflicts-between-macroeconomic-policy-objectives"],"board":"aqa","groups":["macroeconomics"],"keywords":["policy conflicts","unemployment","trade-offs","Phillips curve"],"qpPage":6,"msPage":16,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null}]}
